 and fitness influencer  who i've actually reviewed in the past  so if you want to see that video you can  check it out  right here now in january 2020  sarah launched a brand new app called  sunny  which combines tips for healthy eating  cooking  and naturopathic wellness from a  nutritionist naturopath  and from sarah herself now since the app  came out  i have received a tsunami of dms and  comments  asking me to review it so i download the  app myself to give you my honest  thoughts  now let's start with a general overview  of what the app has to offer  so according to sarah the purpose of the  app is to provide  healthy recipes for a wide range of  dietary needs  like gluten free dairy free vegan low  fodmap etc  so sarah herself has discussed her own  struggles with pcos  food intolerances and hormonal acne and  has testified herself  that changing her diet has been  paramount to her healing journey  so let's start by chatting about the  section called the healthy  plate which was developed in partnership  with a nutritionist  d zibara  [Music]  so dee has an advanced diploma in  nutritionals medicine  but is not a registered dietitian and  actually nutritionist  is not a regulated profession in  australia  but back to the healthy plate this is  essentially sarah's rules for like  portion sizes and building a balanced  meal  now you all know how important balance  is when it comes to  healthy meals and snacks so this part  was particularly  interesting to me now according to sunny  each meal should have one handful of  greens  at least one handful of colorful fruits  and veg  one handful of starchy carbs one palm  size of protein and three  fingers of good fats and one thumb of  superfood sprinkles which include  premium ingredients like maca  cacao powder and acai powder according  to  the smoothie section so although ser is  australian  i compared these suggestions to the ones  outlined in my national food guide  and all in all i would say that the  portions are pretty representative  of the recommendations for healthy  serving sizes  that would help you meet your macro and  micronutrient goals  so that's great however there are a few  things that i  definitely want to flag first of all i  think you all know what i'm going to say  about the  superfood sprinkles thing  probably not necessary if you're already  getting a decent serving of both greens  and colorful fruits and veg in your meal  already not only do i think it's  kind of unnecessary for most people  following the other suggestions in this  plan  but it also renders the diet pretty  inaccessible to a lot of folks out there  more on that of course in a moment now  my other major  issue with the diet recommendations is  that they are made with the assumption  that everyone is both gluten and  dairy-free  so i know that sarah preaches this way  of eating because it has been helpful to  her  however if you don't have known  intolerances  celiac or allergies to dairy and gluten  you do not need to eliminate them from  your diet  next we're going to put our dietitian  hats on and take a closer look at the  nutritional content  of a couple of the recipes offered on  sunny  [Music]  so let's begin with a savory one one  recipe that i saw online getting a lot  of negative buzz  was the avocado carbonara pasta  now unlike traditional carbonara pasta  which is obviously rich in both dairy  and gluten  two no-nos in sarah's books sears  version  uses buckwheat pasta avocado and  nutritional yeast  sounds great to me after doing some  quick nutrition calculations  you can see that one serving of sonny's  avocado carbonara  clocks out at around 669 calories  with 26 grams of protein 77 grams of net  carbs  26 grams of fat 5 grams of saturated fat  and over a thousand milligrams of sodium  now if we compare this to a lightened up  version from stanford healthcare's  website  that contains you know the traditional  cheese and the traditional wheat pasta  we will see sarah's version has more  than twice the calories  four times the fat and twice the  saturated fat  carbs and salt we are getting a little  bit more protein and more healthy  monounsaturated fats from the avocado  which are really important additions so  it's not like one recipe is necessarily  good and the other one is bad  i say that both can be healthy choices  depending on your nutritional goals  and dietary preferences now let's take a  look at one of sunny's  sweet treats so i decided to zero in on  sarah's  white chocolate cheesecake recipe which  is dairy dairy-free  gluten-free and totally vegan versus a  conventional  white chocolate cheesecake recipe i  found online so  sarah's version has over 600 calories 44  grams of carbs  four and a half grams of fiber 23 grams  of sugar  and 46 grams of fat 19 of which  are saturated fat meanwhile a slice of  traditional gluten and dairy-filled  white chocolate cheesecake  contains around the same amount of carbs  sugar  and saturated fat but almost half of the  total fat  and 150 fewer calories yes you're  getting a couple grams more fiber  and the ingredients may be less refined  in sarah's version  but i probably wouldn't rely on  cheesecake to  meet your fiber or your antioxidant  goals  anyways again it really comes down to  your own  dietary needs and preferences i mean  obviously if you're vegan and you're  gluten free  sarah's recipe fits the bill but at the  end of the day  i kind of think that dessert is dessert  i  love creating recipes that cater to  different dietary limitations like  plant-based  or gluten-free but these attributes  don't necessarily  make them healthier than the og  in fact if the healthified version is  less satisfying and enjoyable to you  it may often lead you on what i call a  flavor hunt  looking for what you actually crave and  the result is often  overeating so you know there's a place  for both of these as long as they're  enjoyed  i say if you want cheesecake have a  cheesecake that you  actually want to eat savor it and move  on  now let's dive into the naturopathic  section of the app  which was created in partnership with  the australian naturopath  hayden keys in the app keys shares  specific  naturopathic medical advice related to  very specific health ailments and  conditions so  i did want to kind of like fact check  the evidence on a few  random ones that popped up for me in the  app one of the first ones that i saw was  about how to deal with  always being cold  [Music]  now the app suggests that this is  typically due to poor circulation which  can be true especially when we're  talking about cold hands and  feet what's the app solution well just  add some warming herbs like cinnamon and  ginger to your diet  okay while we do have research to  support cinnamon and ginger in improving  arterial blood flow and circulation this  is  largely being done on animals or in  petri dishes  so we really have no quality evidence  of its usefulness in real life now keys  also suggest getting blood work done to  rule out a thyroid diagnosis from your  doctor  yes yes and yes while some of the  naturopathic medical advice i found on  the app  felt a little random and maybe a little  out of scope for an app like this  speaking to your doctor to rule out a  deficiency or a health condition  should probably have been suggestion  numero  uh excuse my french not like just  putting cinnamon  in your oats or ginger in your tea now  there are also a number of conditions  that sunny doesn't even mention  that could also result in feeling cold  like raynaud's  atherosclerosis and nerve damage and we  can't forget about the  big one that i would expect potentially  from sarah's demographic and that  is under eating one of the most common  symptoms  of disordered eating is feeling cold all  the time  in fact a study comparing a group of  adults fed a  calorie restricted diet with another one  on a typical diet  found that the dieters had significantly  lower  body temperature if someone is on an app  designed by a wellness influencer and  they're chronically cold  the risk for disordered eating should  definitely  be flagged another problematic example  is around sunny's suggestions on  acid reflux  so first sunny instructs readers to cut  out processed foods refined foods  junk food and fast food and argues that  gluten and dairy are  other major triggers to omit this my  friends  is what we call confirmation bias and it  is  deeply ingrained in this app first the  good  we do have research to suggest that for  those who are sensitive  eating an excess of high fat food spicy  food  chocolate and alcohol can exacerbate  acid reflux now although there are  studies suggesting that avoiding gluten  has been proven to be helpful for people  experiencing gerd most of these studies  are  conducted on people with pre-existing  celiac disease diagnosis  who are medically instructed to avoid  gluten  anyways in other words this is  not a recommendation for the masses as  for dairy  studies suggest that for folks without  lactose intolerance  up to three servings of dairy per day  does not appear to trigger  reflux symptoms even when we're looking  at  higher fat options so again assuming  that you  do not have a medical reason to avoid  dairy or gluten  there's little reason that you should be  cutting it out in fact  while this way of eating seems to work  for sarah's needs i might say that it's  slightly irresponsible of sarah  to be putting her personal dietary  restrictions  onto others on mass when these  intolerances  are a very individual thing finally  sunny's final suggestion for acid reflux  is to consume  chamomile ginger licorice and  marshmallow teas  all of which have been associated with  reducing some digestive symptoms  however what they don't tell you is that  you'd probably need to consume  a lot of it to get any kind of relief  for example  in the case of marshmallow tea studies  suggest that you would need like  two spoonfuls of the stuff steeped in  tea  basically every time that you eat is it  dangerous advice to drink tea  no not at all but if you're truly  really suffering you might also need  pharmaceutical support  and again should definitely be speaking  to your doctor about what will work best  for you  finally the last medical issue we'll be  looking at on  sunny is the app's advice around  treating an  abscess  [Music]  seems like a pretty intense medical  condition for a  wellness app to be doling out advice for  but let's see what they say  for those of you who may not know what  an abscess is  maybe put down your snack for a hot  minute because  i'm about to say a pretty gross word  in short because you're gonna want me to  keep this short  it's a concentrated deposit of pus  that forms when there's an infection in  the body  pus oh that is definitely up there with  moist  in the top 10 worst words  in the english language oh my god ew  david  anyway sonny's first suggestion is to up  your intake of dietary zinc  as zinc helps to build up the body's  defenses against infection  now it is true that zinc plays an  important role in supporting the body's  immune system  there's also research to suggest the  success of utilizing certain metals  as a strategy for inhibiting microbial  growth  in abcessed tissue however again  this research has been done on mice  which is just not a sufficient  base for recommendations to the masses  in my books not to mention even if  dietary zinc  did play a clinical role in abscess  reduction  how many pumpkin seeds or oysters would  a girl need to eat  to like cure an infection i mean  sounds like a pretty pricey endeavor to  me  the next recommendation of said abscess  reduction  is to increase your intake of echinacea  as  they suggest that echinacea can help  boost  the immune system first of all nothing  you eat  can boost your immune system nor would  you  necessarily want it to since an  overactive immune system  is just as dangerous as an underactive  one  and while i get that a lot of us have  like accepted acne  as an immune booster and have thought of  that as like a wellness  fact the reality is that the research  looking at the relationship between  echinacea and immunity  is really spotty and also kind of mixed  a lot of information regarding dosage  potency  and long-term effects remain unknown  with some studies  showing no impact of the herb at all  or even negative long-term effects on  immune health  so yeah not sure that that one  holds up lastly sunny advises readers to  use  burdock root tea tree oil and manuka  honey  to treat an abscess now while burdock  has been seen to have some positive  antibacterial effects  on blood in mice there are not really  any quality human studies out there  that substantiate these wellness claims  in terms of tea tree oil  there was one study suggesting that  applying the oil topically could help to  decrease  wound healing time in those with an  abscess and infection  however again the sample size of the  study was very small  so we would definitely need to see more  quality evidence before making this  the go-to treatment finally manuka honey  has  definitely gained a lot of attention in  the wellness community for  its alleged positive role in wound  healing  but again we're talking petri dish  research here folks  not actual living breathing humans  and while results from this kind of in  vitro research  is always super exciting it is just  a jumping off point for more quality  trials  and really should never be the basis of  important medical advice  for something as serious as a bacterial  infection  while some home remedies like these may  have a role in treating  smaller abscesses if that's the plural  you know they often do need specialized  medical attention  to be drained and treated with  antibiotics  i don't know about you but if i had a  pocket of infected  pus somewhere on my body  i would be getting that looked at a sap  not smearing expensive honey all over it  and throwing back  handfuls of seeds next so i just want to  like  close off this section by acknowledging  that yeah i might be a little salty  and sassy here but i don't have  anything against functional medicine or  naturopathics  i have worked with naturopaths  personally and professionally  and i think that they can complement  conventional medicine really  really well and while i give sarah a ton  of credit for putting a disclaimer at  the beginning of this section  that you know it's not intended to be  medical advice and  more so just suggestions for the  purposes of well-being  i am pretty sure that any er doctor  would be horrified to see a patient  present with an  advanced stage abscess an infection who  thought that putting some extra pecans  on their salad  would make it go away so i would rather  see the section  removed or at the very least whittled  down to reflect conditions which we have  more quality research  to support nutrition intervention i want  to finish  off by discussing one of the big  criticisms of the app  it's accessibility or more accurately  it's lack thereof first and foremost  let's talk about the price  [Music]  what's funny to me is that most of you  who asked me to review this app  didn't really care about my thoughts on  the recipes or nutrition  or even the naturopathic advice it was  actually the  deep dent that it left in your bank  account  i'm gonna be real with you guys this app  is  pretty pricey for what you get to break  it down for you  it is 41.99 for three months  64.99 for six months or 104.99  for a year ouch that's a lot  while you do get to download it for free  from the app store  the healthy plate pantry staples cooking  basics  and nourishme features are all locked  until you cough up the dough  as an entrepreneur myself i fully  appreciate the amount of work that  likely went into creating  this app but i think at the very least a  free trial  would have been nice and even after that  i'm not sure that the value is there  when we consider how this content  compares to  everything else that you can access  online for free  at the end of the day though i guess if  you don't like the price  don't buy the app but what bothers me  more  is the kind of ingredients you'll find  under sarah's pantry  staples so this section features  a list of around 40 to 50 different  items that sarah suggests  are necessary purchases and most of them  apparently need to be organic now i've  discussed my thoughts on organics in my  sorel amore video  here but in short while organic foods do  contain  fewer synthetic pesticides and  fertilizers and are free of hormones and  antibiotics  the research suggests that they  generally do not have any huge  nutritional advantages over regular  conventional foods  and the residue levels found on  conventional foods  are well within range for safe  consumption  it's also still unclear if the  pesticides  that are used in organic farming are  significantly safer than  non-synthetic pesticides used in  conventional farming  honestly though whether or not you  choose organics is a totally  personal choice but i'm bringing this up  because  organic food is around 10 to 50 percent  more expensive than conventional food  which again  adds an element of exclusivity to this  app  so if it comes down to eating enough  fruits and vegetables  or eating less but choosing organic  especially if you're already kind of in  debt from downloading this app  i say your priority should be to eat  enough  period quantity in this case is  definitely more important to me than the  organic label  my second issue with the pantry staple  section are the amount of wellness type  products  that have been categorized as staples  insinuating  that these are foods that you absolutely  must eat for good health  for example this list includes  ingredients like agave nectar  apple cider vinegar buckwheat flour  coconut sugar  bone broth concentrate cold pressed hemp  seed oil  raw honey cacao powder and pink  himalayan  sea salt all of these are healthy foods  absolutely  and all great nice to have additions  but even implicitly suggesting that  these are essential for good  health is kind of off-putting to me i  think it's fair to say that most people  either can't afford  all of these specialty ingredients on a  regular basis  or they live in regions without physical  access  to specialty food shops that are  required to purchase them  and like i said earlier if you're eating  a general balanced diet with adequate  fruits and vegetables as this app also  suggests  the need for organic pink himalayan sea  salt  is very quickly rendered obsolete  after doing some quick and dirty math i  calculated that the cost of a one year  subscription  plus purchasing just some of these  specialty pantry items  would put you back about 300 to 400 per  year  and that doesn't even touch your actual  groceries  and meals this is just not realistic  i hate that people would download the  app  shell out their cash on getting past the  paywall  only to be faced with the false  realization that healthy living  is only for the rich the goal should be  to inspire as many people as possible to  make  healthier choices regardless of their  budget while also acknowledging that of  course  socioeconomics present significant  inequalities to people's access to good  health  so while i acknowledge that accessing  individualized care  is a privileged opportunity hence why  so many people turn to youtubers and  influencers  and apps you might be better off  investing that money  in speaking to a registered dietitian or  another certified  healthcare practitioner who can really  help you make  an individualized wellness plan that you  can stick to  and can afford long term so here are my  final thoughts on the sunny app  summarized as some little quick and  dirty pros and cons  because your girl loves a snappy little  list  pros it's a convenient place to find  nutritious  recipes in one spot it offers  reasonable suggestions on portion sizes  and it may be a helpful resource  regarding cooking substitutions  for those with gluten and dairy  restrictions cons  it offers some misleading nutrition  advice  it shares some far-fetched medical  counseling that  feels a little out of scope and it  paints an  elitist view of healthy eating rooted in  privileged wellness culture  all of that being said if the app's  content appeals to you  offers helpful recommendations that work  for you and your lifestyle  and is within budget then by all means  it's probably a really good buy for you  the beautiful thing about the internet  today  is that there is really something for  everyone  but i did just want to make everyone  aware of what they're getting themselves  into  before they hit subscribe and that's all  i hope this video was useful  to help you make an informed decision  about whether or not to purchase  the sunny app thank you again to  skillshare for sponsoring this video  if you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below on  what you'd like to see me review next  subscribe to the channel and i'll see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye

