ody temperature if someone is on an app  designed by a wellness influencer and  they're chronically cold  the risk for disordered eating should  definitely  be flagged another problematic example  is around sunny's suggestions on  acid reflux  so first sunny instructs readers to cut  out processed foods refined foods  junk food and fast food and argues that  gluten and dairy are  other major triggers to omit this my  friends  is what we call confirmation bias and it  is  deeply ingrained in this app first the  good  we do have research to suggest that for  those who are sensitive  eating an excess of high fat food spicy  food  chocolate and alcohol can exacerbate  acid reflux now although there are  studies suggesting that avoiding gluten  has been proven to be helpful for people  experiencing gerd most of these studies  are  conducted on people with pre-existing  celiac disease diagnosis  who are medically instructed to avoid  gluten  anyways in other words this is  not a recommendation for the masses as  for dairy  studies suggest that for folks without  lactose intolerance  up to three servings of dairy per day  does not appear to trigger  reflux symptoms even when we're looking  at  higher fat options so again assuming  that you  do not have a medical reason to avoid  dairy or gluten  there's little reason that you should be  cutting it out in fact  while this way of eating seems to work  for sarah's needs i might say that it's  slightly irresponsible of sarah  to be putting her personal dietary  restrictions  onto others on mass when these  intolerances  are a very individual thing finally  sunny's final suggestion for acid reflux  is to consume  chamomile ginger licorice and  marshmallow teas  all of which have been associated with  reducing some digestive symptoms  however what they don't tell you is that  you'd probably need to consume  a lot of it to get any kind of relief  for example  in the case of marshmallow tea studies  suggest that you would need like  two spoonfuls of the stuff steeped in  tea  basically every time that you eat is it  dangerous advice to drink tea  no not at all but if you're truly  really suffering you might also need  pharmaceutical support  and again should definitely be speaking  to your doctor about what will work best  for you  finally the last medical issue we'll be  looking at on  sunny is the app's advice around  treating an  abscess  [Music]  seems like a pretty intense medical  condition for a  wellness app to be doling out advice for  but let's see what they say  for those of you who may not know what  an abscess is  maybe put down your snack for a hot  minute because  i'm about to say a pretty gross word  in short because you're gonna want me to  keep this short  it's a concentrated deposit of pus  that forms when there's an infection in  the body  pus oh that is definitely up there with  moist  in the top 10 worst words  in the english language oh my god ew  david  anyway sonny's first suggestion is to up  your intake of dietary zinc  as zinc helps to build up the body's  defenses against infection  now it is true that zinc plays an  important role in supporting the body's  immune system  there's also research to suggest the  success of utilizing certain metals  as a strategy for inhibiting microbial  growth  in abcessed tissue however again  this research has been done on mice  which is just not a sufficient  base for recommendations to the masses  in my books not to mention even if  dietary zinc  did play a clinical role in abscess  reduction  how many pumpkin seeds or oysters would  a girl need to eat  to like cure an infection i mean  sounds like a pretty pricey endeavor to  me  the next recommendation of said abscess  reduction  is to increase your intake of echinacea  as  they suggest that echinacea can help  boost  the immune system first of all nothing  you eat  can boost your immune system nor would  you  necessarily want it to since an  overactive immune system  is just as dangerous as an underactive  one  and while i get that a lot of us have  like accepted acne  as an immune booster and have thought of  that as like a wellness  fact the reality is that the research  looking at the relationship between  echinacea and immunity  is really spotty and also kind of mixed  a lot of information regarding dosage  potency  and long-term effects remain unknown  with some studies  showing no impact of the herb at all  or even negative long-term effects on  immune health  so yeah not sure that that one  holds up lastly sunny advises readers to  use  burdock root tea tree oil and manuka  honey  to treat an abscess now while burdock  has been seen to have some positive  antibacterial effects  on blood in mice there are not really  any quality human studies out there  that substantiate these wellness claims  in terms of tea tree oil  there was one study suggesting that  applying the oil topically could help to  decrease  wound healing time in those with an  abscess and infection  however again the sample size of the  study was very small  so we would definitely need to see more  quality evidence before making this  the go-to treatment finally manuka honey  has  definitely gained a lot of attention in  the wellness community for  its alleged positive role in wound  healing  but again we're talking petri dish  research here folks  not actual living breathing humans  and while results from this kind of in  vitro research  is always super exciting it is just  a jumping off point for more quality  trials  and really should never be the basis of  important medical advice  for something as serious as a bacterial  infection  while some home remedies like these may  have a role in treating  smaller abscesses if that's the plural  you know they often do need specialized  medical attention  to be drained and treated with  antibiotics  i don't know about you but if i had a  pocket of infected  pus somewhere on my body  i would be getting that looked at a sap  not smearing expensive honey all over it  and throwing back  handfuls of seeds next so i just want to  like  close off this section by acknowledging  that yeah i might be a little salty  and sassy here but i don't have  anything against functional medicine or  naturopathics  i have worked with naturopaths  personally and professionally  and i think that they can complement  conventional medicine really  really well and while i give sarah a ton  of credit for putting a disclaimer at  the beginning of this section  that you know it's not intended to be  medical advice and  more so just suggestions for the  purposes of well-being  i am pretty sure that any er doctor  would be horrified to see a patient  present with an  advanced stage abscess an infection who  thought that putting some extra pecans  on their salad  would make it go away so i would rather  see the section  removed or at the very least whittled  down to reflect conditions which we have  more quality research  to support nutrition intervention i want  to finish  off by discussing one of the big  criticisms of the app  it's accessibility or more accurately  it's lack thereof first and foremost  let's talk about the price  [Music]  what's funny to me is that most of you  who asked me to review this app  didn't really care about my thoughts on  the recipes or nutrition  or even the naturopathic advice it was  actually the  deep dent that it left in your bank  account  i'm gonna be real with you guys this app  is  pretty pricey for what you get to break  it down for you  it is 41.99 for three months  64.99 for six months or 104.99  for a year ouch that's a lot  while you do get to download it for free  from the app store  the healthy plate pantry staples cooking  basics  and nourishme features are all locked  until you cough up the dough  as an entrepreneur myself i fully  appreciate the amount of work that  likely went into creating  this app but i think at the very least a  free trial  would have been nice and even after that  i'm not sure that the value is there  when we consider how this content  compares to  everything else that you can access  online for free  at the end of the day though i guess if  you don't like the price  don't buy the app but what bothers me  more  is the kind of ingredients you'll find  under sarah's pantry  staples so this section features  a list of around 40 to 50 different  items that sarah suggests  are necessary purchases and most of them  apparently need to be organic now i've  discussed my thoughts on organics in my  sorel amore video  here but in short while organic foods do  contain  fewer synthetic pesticides and  fertilizers and are free of hormones and  antibiotics  the research suggests that they  generally do not have any huge  nutritional advantages over regular  conventional foods  and the residue levels found on  conventional foods  are well within range for safe  consumption  it's also still unclear if the  pesticides  that are used in organic farming are  significantly safer than  non-synthetic pesticides used in  conventional farming  honestly though whether or not you  choose organics is a totally  personal choice but i'm bringing this up  because  organic food is around 10 to 50 percent  more expensive than conventional food  which again  adds an element of exclusivity to this  app  so if it comes down to eating enough  fruits and vegetables  or eating less but choosing organic  especially if you're already kind of in  debt from downloading this app  i say your priority should be to eat  enough  period quantity in this case is  definitely more important to me than the  organic label  my second issue with the pantry staple  section are the amount of wellness type  products  that have been categorized as staples  insinuating  that these are foods that you absolutely  must eat for good health  for example this list includes  ingredients like agave nectar  apple cider vinegar buckwheat flour  coconut sugar  bone broth concentrate cold pressed hemp  seed oil  raw honey cacao powder and pink  himalayan  sea salt all of these are healthy foods  absolutely  and all great nice to have additions  but even implicitly suggesting that  these are essential for good  health is kind of off-putting to me i  think it's fair to say that most people  either can't afford  all of these specialty ingredients on a  regular basis  or they live in regions without physical  access  to specialty food shops that are  required to purchase them  and like i said earlier if you're eating  a general balanced diet with adequate  fruits and vegetables as this app also  suggests  the need for organic pink himalayan sea  salt  is very quickly rendered obsolete  after doing some quick and dirty math i  calculated that the cost of a one year  subscription  plus purchasing just some of these  specialty pantry items  would put you back about 300 to 400 per  year  and that doesn't even touch your actual  groceries  and meals this is just not realistic  i hate that people would download the  app  shell out their cash on getting past the  paywall  only to be faced with the false  realization that healthy living  is only for the rich the goal should be  to inspire as many people as possible to  make  healthier choices regardless of their  budget while also acknowledging that of  course  socioeconomics present significant  inequalities to people's access to good  health  so while i acknowledge that accessing  individualized care  is a privileged opportunity hence why  so many people turn to youtubers and  influencers  and apps you might be better off  investing that money  in speaking to a registered dietitian or  another certified  healthcare practitioner who can really  help you make  an individualized wellness plan that you  can stick to  and can afford long term so here are my  final thoughts on the sunny app  summarized as some little quick and  dirty pros and cons  because your girl loves a snappy little  list  pros it's a convenient place to find  nutritious  recipes in one spot it offers  reasonable suggestions on portion sizes  and it may be a helpful resource  regarding cooking substitutions  for those with gluten and dairy  restrictions cons  it offers some misleading nutrition  advice  it shares some far-fetched medical  counseling that  feels a little out of scope and it  paints an  elitist view of healthy eating rooted in  privileged wellness culture  all of that being said if the app's  content appeals to you  offers helpful recommendations that work  for you and your lifestyle  and is within budget then by all means  it's probably a really good buy for you  the beautiful thing about the internet  today  is that there is really something for  everyone  but i did just want to make everyone  aware of what they're getting themselves  into  before they hit subscribe and that's all  i hope this video was useful  to help you make an informed decision  about whether or not to purchase  the sunny app thank you again to  skillshare for sponsoring this video  if you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below on  what you'd like to see me review next  subscribe to the channel and i'll see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye
 

